@node emacsctl Architecture
@section emacsctl Architecture

emacsctl lives in @file{cmacs/emacsctl/} (@file{ctl-*.c}, @code{Ctl}
GType namespace), builds standalone via its own Makefile (invoked
from @file{src/Makefile.in}), and links @emph{no Emacs objects} ---
just GLib, GObject, GIO, json-glib, yaml-glib, and optionally
readline.

The pipeline for every command:

@example
argv -> CtlApplication -> CtlCommandRegistry -> CtlCommand.run()
            |                                        |
      CtlConfig/CtlContext                     CtlInvocation
      (contexts, aliases)                      |          |
                                        CtlTransport   CtlResult
                                        (D-Bus | ssh)      |
                                                     CtlFormatter
                                                (table|json|yaml|raw)
@end example

GObject is used deliberately: @emph{abstract classes} where behavior
is extended (@code{CtlCommand}, @code{CtlReplRuntime}),
@emph{interfaces} where implementations swap (@code{CtlTransport}
with D-Bus and ssh implementations, @code{CtlFormatter} with four
renderers), @emph{boxed types} for values (@code{CtlResult},
@code{CtlInvocation}, @code{CtlContext}), and @emph{signals} for
events (@code{CtlTransport::closed}, @code{CtlWatcher::event},
@code{CtlCommandRegistry::command-added}).

Nearly every subsystem verb is @emph{one table row} --- a
@code{CtlMethodSpec} naming the CLI path, D-Bus interface/method, a
positional argspec, and a reply kind.  @code{CtlMethodCommand}
parses the argspec, builds the GVariant tuple, calls through
whatever transport the invocation resolved, and maps the reply into
a @code{CtlResult} for the formatter.  Adding a server method to the
CLI is one row; usage strings and completion fall out for free.

The typed interfaces emacsctl consumes are implemented in
@file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-*.c} (@pxref{D-Bus Service}).  The
MCP-parity set (Crispy, Bacon, Eshell, Edit, Input, Debug, Ai,
Gsurf, GnusEye, Podomation, Video, Audio, Speech, Lrg, Instance,
Log) mirrors the MCP tool registry one-to-one, so AI agents (MCP)
and humans/scripts (emacsctl) see the same capability surface.
Interface name constants live in one header,
@file{cmacs/emacsctl/ctl-ifaces.h}.
